1. Based on our first implementation, which of the following apply?
    A. We are coding to concrete implementations, not interfaces.
        - WeatherData depends on the concrete display classes. If those classes change, WeatherData might break
        - Violates Principle: Program to an interface, not an implementation.
    B. For every new display element, we need to alter code.
        - The subject (WeatherData) must be modified every time a new display is added. Adding a new display
          requires modifying existing, stable code (WeatherData), instead of extending behavior through polymorphism.
        - Violates Principle: Open for extension, closed for modification (Open/Closed Principle)
    C. We have no way to add display elements at runtime.
        - Displays are wired in as explicit calls of concrete implementations so we can't
          dynamically subscribe/unsubscribe at runtime.
        - Violates Principle: Favor loose coupling between objects that interact.
    E. We haven't encapsulated what changes.
        - The number and type of displays change and that variability is baked into WeatherData
        - Violates Principle: Identify what varies and encapsulate it from what stays the same.

2. For each design principle, describe how the Observer Pattern makes use of the principle.
    A. Identify the aspects of your application that vary and separate them from what stays the same.
        - This principle is applied by separating the subject from its observers. We knew that each display was going to
          change depending on type, and the number of displays could be increased or decreased in the future. Instead of
          having the WeatherData object update each display whenever a state change occurs, the subject updates all
          registered observers at once. The subject no longer cares who is an observer and adding or removing observers
          no longer changes the subject.
    B. Program to an interface, not an implementation.
        - This principle is applied by coding the WeatherData object to the Subject interface and the displays to the
          Observer and Display interfaces. By programming our displays to the Observer interface, it become much easier
          to update and more loosely coupled from the subject.
    C. Favor composition over inheritance.
        - Instead of the observers inherited state from the subject, they form a "HAS-A" relationship with the subject.
          Now the subject has an array composed of many different observers.